[3] exact Onsager cross-check of the strip:  m_lat = 2|K - K*|,  K* = -1/2 log tanh K
    beta= 0.10  strip=2.105911  Onsager=2.105911  diff=+5.41e-12
    beta= 0.15  strip=1.604581  Onsager=1.604581  diff=+1.66e-09
    beta= 0.20  strip=1.222648  Onsager=1.222648  diff=+1.29e-07
    beta= 0.25  strip=0.906834  Onsager=0.906829  diff=+4.67e-06
    beta= 0.30  strip=0.633459  Onsager=0.633358  diff=+1.01e-04
    beta= 0.32  strip=0.533083  Onsager=0.532775  diff=+3.08e-04
    beta= 0.34  strip=0.437225  Onsager=0.436336  diff=+8.89e-04
    (agreement to ~1e-3: the W=12 strip is already the bulk mass at these beta)

[4] reading
    * in the whole disordered window  m_tree < m_lat  (tree is the WORST case:
      more paths, slower decay, smaller mass); the tree gap closes FIRST.
    * beta_c^tree = 0.3466 < beta_c^lat = 0.4407 : the tree orders EARLIER,
      i.e. at fixed beta it is CLOSER to criticality and therefore LESS massive.
    * this is the OPPOSITE sign to the Gaussian model (bc_gaussian.py: m_tree > m_lat).
    * consequence: any 'tree -> lattice' inequality is a statement about the FKG /
      positive-association class, not about connectivity; SU(N) Wilson has no FKG.
